                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $p=P(A)$, so $P(B)=1-p$. Add the probabilities of winning through each mode.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2}{3}&=\\frac{2}{5}p+\\frac{4}{5}(1-p)\\\\\\frac{2}{3}&=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}p\\\\\\frac{2}{5}p&=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}\\\\\\frac{2}{5}p&=\\frac{2}{15}\\\\p&=\\frac{1}{3}\\\\P(B)&=1-\\frac{1}{3}\\\\P(B)&=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(B)=\\dfrac{2}{3}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
